WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680
Film Courage: Wendy we had this comment come in a
couple days ago on YouTube and the

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.179
viewer asked 'What should writers be
concerned with when revising their

00:00:09.179 --> 00:00:16.350
second or third draft of something?' Wendy Kram, Owner of Wendy's LA4Hire: A
really good question because I know like

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:21.359
we all talked about a first draft but
invariably the biggest part of writing

00:00:21.359 --> 00:00:25.859
your script is the rewrite in the
rewrite and the rewrite I think the

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:34.110
biggest danger is well put it this way
the purpose and the benefit is that it's

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:42.090
going to get clearer and crisper and
better and tighter so the danger is when

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:47.190
you lose sight of what your core vision
is now there are two ways this could

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:53.360
happen first if you've never haven't had
a strong core vision to begin with then

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:59.160
each draft the goal should be to refine
what that is and maybe the writer needs

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:04.559
to do an outline first and be really
clear and I do recommend I think Blake

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:10.470
Snyder has a very effective beat sheet
that's a useful tool and again I tell

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:17.130
writers that use it as a general guide
and a general map but don't feel that

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:23.520
you have to adhere to like oh I've got
to insert this moment at exactly this

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:30.329
page or try to contrive things you must
be organic to your follow your own

00:01:30.329 --> 00:01:36.869
organic process the another thing just
with like about rules and following

00:01:36.869 --> 00:01:42.270
formulas I'd like to use as an example
when you see a great prima ballerina

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:48.170
like I saw many years ago
Makarova who was one of the preeminent

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:54.659
ballerinas when I was growing up and she
you know that she practiced her bars and

00:01:54.659 --> 00:01:59.460
she knew her craft but when she got on
stage you then abandon it it becomes

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:04.290
second nature that you should know your
craft but that you can be free enough to

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:09.090
let the muse flow through you if you're
so manufactured where you're trying to

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:13.760
hit every plot point and you're writing
from

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:19.220
that place you're not going to have the
magic and the kind of flow that a writer

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:26.989
needs to have to really create magical
characters and dialogue and story so but

00:02:26.989 --> 00:02:31.610
it's helpful to have this map so this is
for a writer who might not have core

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:36.079
vision to begin with for most writers
they do have a core vision of their

00:02:36.079 --> 00:02:44.870
piece and the danger becomes when you
lose your Center and you start listening

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:53.569
to you're like a weed in the wind that
you keep swaying so this person said I

00:02:53.569 --> 00:02:59.930
should do this so I'm going to do that
this person said you keep going just in

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:06.260
too many different directions it is an
art to be able arter a balance to be

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:14.690
able to take advice feedback suggestions
from other people let it go through a

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:19.549
sieve earth-like filter and something
that occurs to me is to maybe approach

00:03:19.549 --> 00:03:23.959
the advice the way they make
recommendations with meditation when

00:03:23.959 --> 00:03:31.069
you're when thoughts come to you it's
not that you're so rigid about I can't

00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:34.340
think any thoughts I can't think any
thoughts so the same way you're not

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:38.389
being richer than that I'm not going to
take anyone suggestions and you're not

00:03:38.389 --> 00:03:44.569
saying I'm gonna take everyone's
suggestions you okay let's see this was

00:03:44.569 --> 00:03:49.010
a good idea I'm gonna let that percolate
this idea I don't think it really fits

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:52.669
in with the vision so I'm gonna put that
one aside

00:03:52.669 --> 00:03:57.949
I'll reexamine it just in case there's
something there I need to look at and

00:03:57.949 --> 00:04:03.470
there are others that you may go oh this
person they don't get it at all

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:08.239
maybe I'm doing something that's not
making it clear enough but I don't I

00:04:08.239 --> 00:04:13.569
don't think they're the right person to
give me advice for this you cannot a you

00:04:13.569 --> 00:04:19.010
can't be a pleaser where you're trying
to please everybody but generally

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:25.219
speaking if there is valuable feedback
then to take it in and put it through

00:04:25.219 --> 00:04:30.110
your own filter and prod
Sisson see how you can address so the

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:36.650
biggest danger is losing sense of self
and trying to please everybody and then

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:42.410
you wind up with mush and that's nothing
it's neither here and are there there's

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:50.030
no vision so can we just define
coalition here would be a really good

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:58.400
example I had a client who had a
one-hour drama that was inspired by his

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:05.600
life experiences and he had a really
really interesting story based on his

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:11.030
profession and he sent me the draft he
had told me the log line and what he had

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:16.220
done and then I read the draft and I was
like this doesn't sound at all like your

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:22.849
story and he had changed the protagonist
to a woman which can be fine but there

00:05:22.849 --> 00:05:28.400
were it felt so manufactured and
contrived and so I just said a very

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:33.320
innocent question I said I'm really
curious because what you would pitch to

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:39.560
me based on your life experience was so
fascinating and riveting and I felt like

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:45.289
and that's what really interested me
about the idea for this pilot and I

00:05:45.289 --> 00:05:49.580
didn't see what I know of you I didn't
see any of you in this pilot whether it

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:53.949
was a male or female character and he
told me that he had taken a number of

00:05:53.949 --> 00:06:00.500
writing the workshops were or script
coverage services and certainly there

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:07.190
are good ones out there but one they
said you should change it to a female

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:11.750
you should make the protagonist a woman
not a man someone else said oh you

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:17.060
should give her this backstory and you
should give her such and such kind of

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:22.820
relationship with her mother and with an
abusive boyfriend and it became so far

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:28.400
removed from what I thought was really
the core vision or essence of the story

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:34.669
that he wanted to tell and he let go of
that and came up with something that and

00:06:34.669 --> 00:06:39.110
I he said I didn't even want to write
this but I thought I should you know

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:45.229
that these were people who were telling
me that I that I should so that's what I

00:06:45.229 --> 00:06:52.039
think you need to stick with your core
vision that would be an example it's a

00:06:52.039 --> 00:06:57.229
great place to start on we talked about
being able to take constructive

00:06:57.229 --> 00:07:01.879
criticism and not being defensive and
being open to the notes what if people

00:07:01.879 --> 00:07:06.589
are giving you super nice feedback about
things and it feels disingenuous and

00:07:06.589 --> 00:07:10.909
you're not sure is this just a person
that's trying to please me mm-hmm it

00:07:10.909 --> 00:07:14.449
doesn't even have to be a family member
or friend and how do we see through that

00:07:14.449 --> 00:07:23.180
good question I think one is to say when
you're seeking constructive criticism

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:27.860
because it is coming from a I mean I
this is how I'd like to give notes and

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:32.569
if I'm asking someone to evaluate
something for me I may say I need you to

00:07:32.569 --> 00:07:36.949
be gentle
this is my style I don't respond well to

00:07:36.949 --> 00:07:44.319
someone who's going to shred everything
but I do want honest feedback so if

00:07:44.319 --> 00:07:48.979
someone a lot of writers will say to me
and I usually say before I do consulting

00:07:48.979 --> 00:07:55.999
I say I want to make sure that you want
honest feedback certainly everyone has

00:07:55.999 --> 00:08:03.589
an opinion what you will get from me is
an informed opinion being that I am I do

00:08:03.589 --> 00:08:07.669
feel it as one of my strengths as we get
older I think we come to know what some

00:08:07.669 --> 00:08:15.979
of our strengths are my affinity for
literature from a young age to you know

00:08:15.979 --> 00:08:21.889
working in the business and working for
a long time with writers and loving the

00:08:21.889 --> 00:08:29.029
process and that when you as a writer
when you ask an opinion that you a lot

00:08:29.029 --> 00:08:34.519
of regulars will say to me I want an
honest I will don't be afraid you can be

00:08:34.519 --> 00:08:39.380
brutal etc it's not my desire or style
to be brutal because I don't like my

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:46.130
teachers I did not feel that's
constructive but I do feel that I also

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:49.160
